  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 PM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nWe remain under the influence of the large upper ridge parked \nover the southeastern US, which is sponsoring the dry and \nanomalously hot October days we are currently experiencing. \nTemperatures will be approaching, or in some cases likely \nexceeding, daily record highs both today and Thursday.\n\nRecord highs today and Thursday: \nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge                 \n10-14   87(1954)       87(1881)       85(1954)       85(1954)       \n10-15   85(1950)       87(1897)       83(1971)       83(2021)  \n\nA weak cold front will sag southward toward our northern areas as \nweak mid/upper level short wave energy skirts by to our north \ntonight and Thursday, but the front will likely stall to our \nnorth and then retreat north. We may see a few showers mainly \nnorth tonight and Thursday closer to the front. Ensemble data \nshows very limited CAPE, so chances for any thunder look quite \nlow. \n\nAn upper trough digging in out of Canada will push a much stronger \ncold front through our area Friday. This front will not bring much \nin the way of rainfall totals as moisture looks limited and \nshallow, but we will see mostly light showers especially north and\nmountains Friday afternoon and Friday night, with a few light \nshowers possibly lingering into Saturday morning. Winds will be \ngusty Friday from the southwest becoming west and northwest \nlater in the day.\n\nSurface high pressure will build in along with colder and drier air \nfor the weekend. This colder airmass will bring night time \ntemperatures into the range where frost may occur especially \nacross our northern areas. Right now the best chance for \nwidespread frost looks to be Saturday night across the northern \nhalf of our area, and a few outlying valley areas across our \nsouthern half could see patchy frost as well. Frost potential for \nSunday night looks lower, but patchy frost may occur again \nespecially north. \n\nAfter a cool day Saturday with well below normal high temperatures, \nwe will see a warming trend through next Wednesday with dry \nconditions continuing.",
